@page "/five-minutes"

<PageTitle>Five minutes · Linq2Dashboard</PageTitle>

<h1>Try it in five minutes</h1>

<p>
    A dashboard over a hand-written list of orders, in a fresh Blazor app, with nothing to download. Every step is a
    copy-paste. When it runs, replace the list with your own collection and keep the rest.
</p>

<h2>1. A new app and the two packages</h2>

<p>A Blazor Web App with server interactivity, the two Linq2Dashboard packages and <code>HybridCache</code>, which the service in step 3 uses. The commands are the same on Windows, macOS and Linux.</p>

<pre><code>@CreateAppExample</code></pre>

<h2>2. The rows</h2>

<p>Add <code>Order.cs</code> at the project root. A record and a dozen rows are enough to see every facet do something. Note the order without a country: null is a value and shows up as one.</p>

<pre><code>@RowsExample</code></pre>

<h2>3. The service</h2>

<p>
    Add <code>DashboardService.cs</code> at the project root. Real data is not in memory at startup, so the service loads the rows
    and builds the dashboard the first time a page asks, and caches the result. The dashboard is immutable and thread-safe, so one
    instance serves every user until the entry expires; the next request after that builds a new one over fresh rows.
    <code>HybridCache</code> does the hard parts: concurrent first requests share one build, and a failed load is not cached.
    The <code>DisableDistributedCache</code> flag matters: a dashboard is an in-memory index and cannot be serialised, so it must
    never be written to a distributed cache, even when the app later gets one.
</p>

<pre><code>@ServiceExample</code></pre>

<p>Register the service as a singleton in <code>Program.cs</code>, next to the existing service calls.</p>

<pre><code>@ProgramExample</code></pre>

<h2>4. The usings</h2>

<p>Append two lines to <code>Components/_Imports.razor</code> so every page sees the engine and the components.</p>

<pre><code>@ImportsExample</code></pre>

<h2>5. The page</h2>

<p>
    Replace the contents of <code>Components/Pages/Home.razor</code>. The page asks the service for the dashboard when it
    initialises and shows a line of text until it arrives. The line after the route is the one people forget:
    without <code>@@rendermode InteractiveServer</code> the page renders once and clicks do nothing.
</p>

<pre><code>@PageExample</code></pre>

<p>
    The template's <code>App.razor</code> already links <code>Shop.styles.css</code>, the app's scoped-CSS bundle. The components' styles
    are bundled into it, so there is no stylesheet or script to add.
</p>

<h2>6. Run it</h2>

<pre><code>dotnet run</code></pre>

<p>
    Open the address it prints. The first request waits a second for the stand-in load, then the dashboard appears. Reload the
    page: it is instant, because the cache hands out the dashboard it already built. Blazor also renders the page twice, once
    for the first HTML and once when the circuit connects; the second call gets the cached dashboard too.
</p>

<p>
    Click <em>SE</em> under Country: the other countries keep their counts, Status and Amount narrow to
    Swedish orders, the tiles and the table follow, and the URL gains <code>?Country=SE</code>. Click the null value to see the order
    with no country. Remove the chip under the tiles to clear it.
</p>

<h2>What to change next</h2>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Your data.</strong> Replace the body of <code>LoadOrdersAsync</code> with your query, and the selectors with your properties. Anything that yields an <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> works. Build takes about a second per million rows.</li>
    <li><strong>The lifetime.</strong> Ten minutes is a placeholder. Make it as long as your data stays useful, and call <code>InvalidateAsync</code> from the code that changes the orders so the next request rebuilds at once. If a rebuild must not make anyone wait, build the next one in the background and swap it in when it is ready: <code>DashboardView</code> follows a new <code>Dashboard</code> parameter and keeps the selections.</li>
    <li><strong>More facets and metrics.</strong> The <a href="getting-started">getting started</a> page shows the whole builder: labels, top-N with search, date presets, text search, calculated metrics.</li>
    <li><strong>Layout and styling.</strong> Every component takes <code>Class</code> and every colour and size is a <code>--l2d-*</code> custom property. The <a href="usage">usage</a> guide lists them and the rules that surprise.</li>
    <li><strong>A larger starting point.</strong> The <a href="https://github.com/joadan/Linq2Dashboard/tree/master/samples/Linq2Dashboard.Sample" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sample project</a> in the repository is the same wiring over 200 000 generated rows, with scopes, virtualised results and a custom formatter.</li>
</ul>
